<article class="ltx_document"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:4.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Guided by the Experts: Provable Feature Learning Dynamic of Soft-Routed Mixture-of-Experts</span></p>
</div><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:487.8pt;"><span id="p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
<span id="p2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="p2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="p2.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="p2.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fangshuo Liao</span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span>        <span id="p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
<span id="p2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="p2.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="p2.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="p2.1.1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anastasios Kyrillidis</span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:487.8pt;"><span id="p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;">
<span id="p3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="p3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="p3.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Rice University</span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures have emerged as a cornerstone of modern AI systems.
In particular, MoEs route inputs dynamically to specialized experts whose outputs are aggregated through weighted summation. Despite their widespread application, theoretical understanding of MoE training dynamics remains limited to either separate expert-router optimization or only top-1 routing scenarios with carefully constructed datasets.
This paper advances MoE theory by providing convergence guarantees for joint training of soft-routed MoE models with non-linear routers and experts in a student-teacher framework.
We prove that, with moderate over-parameterization, the student network undergoes a feature learning phase, where the router’s learning process is “guided” by the experts, that recovers the teacher’s parameters.
Moreover, we show that a post-training pruning can effectively eliminate redundant neurons, followed by a provably convergent fine-tuning process that reaches global optimality.
To our knowledge, our analysis is the first to bring novel insights in understanding the optimization landscape of the MoE architecture.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>INTRODUCTION</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures have become a fundamental building block in modern artificial intelligence systems, enabling significant advances in model capacity without corresponding increases in computational costs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shazeer et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fedus et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
At its core, a MoE system treats a complicated task as a combination of multiple simpler tasks, which can be handled efficiently by smaller models.
In concept, the conditional routing of the input to different sub-modules of the MoE allows each sub-module to ‘‘specialize’’ in its own domain, leading to an effective decoupling of the overall task complexity.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          In this paper we consider a “classic” MoE instead of the MoE used to increase parameterization without increasing computational costs such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fedus et al. (2022)</a></cite>.</span></span></span>
This approach has achieved remarkable success in large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fedus et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Riquelme et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and multi-modal agentic systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mustafa et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, where conditional computation provides an efficient way to scale model capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The fundamental structure of an MoE layer consists of a set of expert networks (the “experts”) and a gating network that determines the contribution of each expert to the final output.
While simple, this architecture presents theoretical challenges, particularly regarding the joint optimization of both components.
The gating function, typically implemented using a softmax activation, introduces non-convexity that makes the analysis difficult.
This is further complicated by the interplay between expert specialization and router assignments: experts must specialize in certain inputs, while the router must correctly identify the correct combination of experts appropriate for each input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the widespread adoption of MoE architectures in practice, such a theoretical understanding of their optimization dynamic remains limited; see Related Works section.
Existing theoretical work has focused on either simplified linear models or has analyzed the experts and gating networks separately; e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kawata et al. (2025)</a></cite> study the setting where the experts are trained first with the router parameter fixed, followed by a fine-tuning stage of the router itself.
While such a setting simplifies the analysis by decoupling the updates of the router and expert parameters, it deviates from the more beneficial scenario, where a joint optimization is applied to handle intricate task combinations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2022</a>)</cite> studies the joint optimization of the expert and router parameters in a top-1 routed MoE on patched input data, thus reducing the interference between the learning process of the experts in each gradient step.
Although top-1 routing has been a popular approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fedus et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, most state-of-the-art language models such as Mixtral 8x7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, DeepSeek-V3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and Qwen 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> uses a top-<math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> routing with <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to gaps between theory and practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In general, it remains open to study the optimization dynamics of MoEs with more than one activated experts, where experts and router are jointly trained.
This gap is increasingly significant as MoE architectures become fundamental components in state-of-the-art AI systems.
Analysis of these dynamics would not only inform architectural improvements but also provide formal guarantees about model behavior and performance.
Moreover, such an analysis could help us understand better Agentic AI systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, where component orchestration mirrors MoE routing mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These systems must dynamically select appropriate specialized modules (tools, APIs, or reasoning components) based on input context—functionally analogous to expert selection in MoE architectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> Given the difficulty of the task, we focus on the learning of a MoE model with one-layer sigmoid router and non-linear experts over the mean-square-error (MSE) loss in a teacher-student set-up on high dimensional Gaussian input.
In particular, we show that, with moderate over-parameterization and under the gradient flow training, the student MoE enjoys a near-perfect recovery of the feature from the teacher model’s in a sequential order in <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time, where <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimensionality of the input.
Moreover, after the feature learning stage, a greedy pruning can be applied to remove the unused experts.
Lastly, the post-pruning fine-tuning of the student model converges to zero loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notations.</span> Without further specification, we use regular lower-case letters (e.g. <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>) to denote scalars, bold-face lower-case letters (e.g. <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>) to denote vectors, and bold-face capital letters (e.g. <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>) to denote matrices. We use <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}\left(\mu,\sigma^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}\left(\mu,\sigma^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the the Gaussian distribution with mean <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and (co)variance <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a function <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}(x),f^{\prime\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(x),f^{\prime\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime\prime\prime}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime\prime\prime}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote its first three order derivatives, and <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(a)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(a)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote is arbitray <math id="S1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>th order derivative. We use <math id="S1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{poly}\left(x_{1},\dots,x_{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>poly</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{poly}\left(x_{1},\dots,x_{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the polynomial dependency in terms of <math id="S1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dots,x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dots,x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>RELATED WORKS</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theory of Mixture-of-Experts.</span> From an optimization perspective, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2022)</a></cite> studies the convergence rate of top-1 MoE with CNN experts on patched input data. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury et al. (2023)</a></cite> studies the patch-level routing under both the setting with a separately trained expert and router, and the setting of pre-trained experts. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury et al. (2024)</a></cite> shows the pruning effectiveness after fine-tuning a pre-trained MoE model. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kawata et al. (2025)</a></cite> considers the training both a top-1 MoE and a ReLU routed MoE, but under a four-stage training algorithm. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fruytier et al. (2025)</a></cite> studies the convergence of the Expectation-Maximization algorithm for learning MoE. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite> studies the optimization of MoE in a continual learning set-up. From the perspective of sample complexity, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2024c)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2025)</a></cite> studies the sample complexity of correctly identifying experts for softmax MoE under both the logistic loss and the MSE loss. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2024b)</a></cite> shows that sigmoid gated MoEs enjoy a better sample complexity compared with softmax gated MoEs. Other works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kratsios et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and E, 2025</a>)</cite> studies MoE under operator learning, and the expressive power of MoEs, respectively. Following the expressivity line of work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boix-Adsera and Rigollet (2025)</a></cite> studies how the granularity of the experts affects the expressive power of MoEs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature Learning of Neural Networks.</span> As its name suggested, the feature learning framework explores the ability of the neural network to learn intrinsic features of the dataset, which is an ability not present in the traditional Neural Tangent Kernel framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacot et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. In particular <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2023)</a></cite> studies the hidden-neuron evolution during training, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Damian et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mousavi-Hosseini et al. (2023a)</a></cite> investigates how gradient-based learning discovers the intrinsic low-dimensional subspace of data. Along this line of work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mousavi-Hosseini et al. (2023b)</a></cite> studies the learning with data sampled from distribution with a spiked covariance matrix. Recently, a popular line of work studies the learning of Gaussian single/multi-index models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bietti et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bietti et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Şimşek et al., 2025</a>)</cite> Noticeably, this line of work adopts the Hermite expansion of the nonlinear function to transform the loss objective into a form similar to the tensor decomposition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. In terms of the proof technique, our work is similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. (2025)</a></cite> by utilizing the sharp phase transition that occurs from the high information exponent of the activation function.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07205v1/router_expert_align_heatmap.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:673/269;" width="673" height="269" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Training MoE in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) on (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) with <math id="S2.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\star}=5,m=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\star}=5,m=25</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1000</annotation></semantics></math> with online batch SGD simulating GF on the population loss. Left: alignment values of the router parameters <math id="S2.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: alignment values of the expert parameters <math id="S2.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Student model.</span> In this paper, we consider the training of a normalized MoE with <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> experts. In particular, given inputs <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we study the setting of a one-layer router with parameter <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{V}}}{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{V}}}{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\left(\cdot\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\left(\cdot\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is an entry-wise sigmoid function, and <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{V}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{V}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the row-wise normalized version of <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In short, we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{V}}}{\mathbf{x}}\right)_{i}:=\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\pi\left(\frac{{\mathbf{v}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}}{\left\|{\mathbf{v}}_{i}\right\|_{2}}\right);\;\forall i\in[m]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{V}}}{\mathbf{x}}\right)_{i}:=\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\pi\left(\frac{{\mathbf{v}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}}{\left\|{\mathbf{v}}_{i}\right\|_{2}}\right);\;\forall i\in[m]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{v}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{v}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th row of <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider each expert as a one-layer non-linear function with parameter <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sigma\left(\frac{{\mathbf{w}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}}{\left\|{\mathbf{w}}_{i}\right\|_{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sigma\left(\frac{{\mathbf{w}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}}{\left\|{\mathbf{w}}_{i}\right\|_{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we choose <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\left(a\right)=a^{3}-3a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\left(a\right)=a^{3}-3a</annotation></semantics></math> to be the third-order Hermite polynomial. Letting <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}=\left\{\left({\mathbf{v}}_{i},{\mathbf{w}}_{i}\right)\right\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}=\left\{\left({\mathbf{v}}_{i},{\mathbf{w}}_{i}\right)\right\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the student model is given by</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right):=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right):=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Our choice of the sigmoid router is motivated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2024b)</a></cite> which shows that sigmoid routing is more sample efficient than softmax routing. Moreover, using Hermite polynomial as the activation function has been a popular approach in previous study of the feature learning mechanism of neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arous et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Lastly, the choice of normalizing the weights is also a popular choice in prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data and Teacher Model.</span> The teacher model <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> we consider has the same structure as in the the student model, but with <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> experts. In addition, we assume that the parameter of the teacher model’s parameters <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{1}^{\star},\dots,\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{m^{\star}}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}^{\star},\dots,\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{m^{\star}}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{1}^{\star},\dots,\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{m^{\star}}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}^{\star},\dots,\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{m^{\star}}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> forms an orthonormal list. We assume that the input data comes from a standard Gaussian distribution <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},{\mathbf{I}}_{d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},{\mathbf{I}}_{d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, and labels are generated according to</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right):=\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>⋆</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>⋆</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right):=\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, this set-up implies that the input space <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is softly partitioned by the teacher’s router. The goal of the student model is to learn both the features <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>s that gives a correct partitions, and the features <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>s that leads to the effective specialization of experts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We consider training the student model <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> on the population mean-squared error (MSE) loss <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> using gradient flow <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\bm{\theta}(t)=-\nabla{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}(t)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\bm{\theta}(t)=-\nabla{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}(t)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> over the data distribution defined by the teacher model. To be more specific, the MSE has the form</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}},y}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-y\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-y\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-y\right)^{2}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}},y}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-y\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-y\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-y\right)^{2}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E3Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},{\mathbf{I}}_{d}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},{\mathbf{I}}_{d}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">We initialize the student model by <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(0),{\mathbf{w}}_{i}(0)\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},d^{-1}{\mathbf{I}}_{d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(0),{\mathbf{w}}_{i}(0)\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},d^{-1}{\mathbf{I}}_{d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{v}}_{i}(0)=\hat{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(0)-\hat{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(0)\cdot\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{v}}_{i}(0)=\hat{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(0)-\hat{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(0)\cdot\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> to decouple the router and expert weights.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          The behavior that <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{v}}_{i}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{i}(t)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{v}}_{i}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{i}(t)=0</annotation></semantics></math> does not hold throughout gradient flow training.</span></span></span> We also need to make the following assumption on the sigmoid function, which is numerically checked in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Plotting Sigmoid Property" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmasump1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_asump">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmasump1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmasump1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmasump1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmasump1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasump1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmasump1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1},z_{2}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1},z_{2}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)</annotation></semantics></math> with arbitrary covariance <math id="Thmasump1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cov}\left(z_{1},z_{2}\right)\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Cov</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cov}\left(z_{1},z_{2}\right)\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="Thmasump1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{z_{1},z_{2}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi^{\prime}(z_{1})\pi^{(3)}(z_{2})}\right.}{\pi^{\prime}(z_{1})\pi^{(3)}(z_{2})}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi^{\prime}(z_{1})\pi^{(3)}(z_{2})}\right]}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{z_{1},z_{2}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi^{\prime}(z_{1})\pi^{(3)}(z_{2})}\right.}{\pi^{\prime}(z_{1})\pi^{(3)}(z_{2})}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi^{\prime}(z_{1})\pi^{(3)}(z_{2})}\right]}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically verify that this setting allows that each of the <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> experts and corresponding router parameter in the teacher model can be recovered by one and only one expert and corresponding router in the student model. According to Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 RELATED WORKS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, for each <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> in the teacher (x-axis), there is one and only one expert and router <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> that converges to <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> (lighter color indicates that <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> are closer to one.)</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the main result of the feature learning phase. Recall the set-up of the student and teacher MoE models in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). An ideal feature learning result would be that, for each router-expert pair <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in the teacher model, there is an exclusive router-expert pair <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> that converges to it. The theorem below states that the matching between the router-expert pair from the teacher model and the router-expert pair from the student model happens in a sequential order.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider training the MoE model <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with respect to a teacher model given by (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) using the gradient flow on the population MSE loss in (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\in(0,\nicefrac{{1}}{{7}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mn mathsize="0.700em">7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\in(0,\nicefrac{{1}}{{7}})</annotation></semantics></math> be given. If <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\Omega\left(m^{\star}\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\Omega\left(m^{\star}\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq\text{poly}\left(m,\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{-1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">poly</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq\text{poly}\left(m,\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{-1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists an injective mapping <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}:[m^{\star}]\rightarrow[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}:[m^{\star}]\rightarrow[m]</annotation></semantics></math> and time steps <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq T_{1}\leq\dots\leq T_{m^{\star}}\leq T^{\star}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq T_{1}\leq\dots\leq T_{m^{\star}}\leq T^{\star}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[m^{\star}-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[m^{\star}-1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T_{\ell},T_{\ell+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T_{\ell},T_{\ell+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Recovered expert-router pairs) </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\geq 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\geq 0.9</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\geq 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\geq 0.9</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Unrecovered expert-router pairs) For all </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\left\{\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right|\right\}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\left\{\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right|\right\}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, for <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\star}\leq t\leq T^{\star}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}{m^{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\star}\leq t\leq T^{\star}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}{m^{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Learned features) </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(T)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\geq 1-\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{7}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{3}d^{\frac{3}{2}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}(T)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\geq 1-\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{7}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{3}d^{\frac{3}{2}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m^{\star}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m^{\star}]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Unused expert-router pairs) for all </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{I}\left([m^{\star}]\right),i_{2}\in[m],i_{1}\neq i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{I}\left([m^{\star}]\right),i_{2}\in[m],i_{1}\neq i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[m^{\star}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[m^{\star}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the following quantities</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip-9.95863pt\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}}(t)\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}(t)\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{2}}(t)\right|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip-9.95863pt\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}}(t)\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}(t)\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{2}}(t)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip-9.95863pt\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{2}}(t)\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{i}}(t)\right|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip-9.95863pt\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{2}}(t)\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{i}}(t)\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip-9.95863pt\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip-9.95863pt\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">are all upper bounded by </span><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> states the result that the MoE training in our set-up undergoes a sequential feature learning phase. As requirements of the theorem, we need <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> to be as large as <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\left(m^{\star}\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\left(m^{\star}\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure that at initialization , at least one of the router-expert pair from the student model have a good enough alignment for each router-expert pair in the teacher. Also we require <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> to be polynomially large in terms of <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to control the interference between the convergence of each router-expert pair, as well as between the convergence of the router parameter and the expert parameter. Due to the polynomial scaling of <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, quantities <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{7}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{3}d^{\frac{3}{2}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{7}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{3}d^{\frac{3}{2}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> are in general small, and <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}{m^{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}{m^{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is large.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the set-up of the theorem, the mapping <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> establishes the correct matching between the expert-router pair in the teacher model and the router-expert pair in the student model. Ideally, we expect <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> to converge to <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Two key points of the theorem are outlined below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequential weak recovery.</span> The first part of the theorem states that such converges happens in a sequential order. By its set-up, <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the time where the first <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> pairs of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math> just achieved a weak convergence to <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> by achieving an inner product with of at least <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9</annotation></semantics></math>. In the mean time, before <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> reaches <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, all the remaining pairs <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math> still stays within a small alignment value with their reference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Near-perfect recovery.</span>The second part of the theorem shows that for any time <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> that exceeds some <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\star}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\star}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> but stays under <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\star}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}{m^{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\star}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}{m^{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, the learned features have converged to inner product values of at least <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{7}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}d^{\frac{3}{2}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{7}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}d^{\frac{3}{2}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the meantime, all the router and expert parameters in the student model that did not converge to any teacher’s parameter must stay nearly orthogonal both to the teacher model’s parameter and to each other. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we will utilize this property to prove the theoretical guarantee of pruning these unused experts in the student model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07205v1/Dynamics.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:606/454;" width="606" height="454"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Dynamics of the routers’ and experts’ alignment value with the teacher’s parameter under the same set-up as Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 RELATED WORKS" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The green curve denotes the loss value. Except for the green curve, dashed line and solid line of the same color denotes a pair of router and expert alignment value.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Guided by the Experts: A Proof Sketch of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will discuss the difficulties and techniques arises in the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hermite expansion of the loss and gradient.</span> The starting point of our proof relies on the Hermite expansion of non-linear functions to study its property with Gaussian inputs. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="He_{k}\left(x\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">He_{k}\left(x\right)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>th-order probabilist’s Hermite polynomial. It is known that the set of Hermite polynomials <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{He_{k}\left(x\right)\right\}_{k=0}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{He_{k}\left(x\right)\right\}_{k=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> consists an basis of the square integrable functions under the Gaussian measure. Therefore, we can expand the sigmoid function as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\left(x\right)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{c_{k}}{k!}He_{k}\left(x\right);\;c_{k}=\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{x\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi^{(k)}\left(x\right)}\right.}{\pi^{(k)}\left(x\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi^{(k)}\left(x\right)}\right]}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\left(x\right)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{c_{k}}{k!}He_{k}\left(x\right);\;c_{k}=\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{x\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi^{(k)}\left(x\right)}\right.}{\pi^{(k)}\left(x\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi^{(k)}\left(x\right)}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are the Hermite coefficients of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\left(x\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\left(x\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\left(x\right)=He_{3}\left(x\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\left(x\right)=He_{3}\left(x\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> has the form</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{c_{k}}{k!}He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{c_{k}}{k!}He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{c_{k}}{k!}He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>⋆</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>⋆</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{c_{k}}{k!}He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Carrying this idea to the setting of minimizing the MSE in <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we notice that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}-\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}-\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad+\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad+\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where one could notice that the last term does not depend on <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the first two terms involves second-order terms on <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. As an illustration, we expand the first term as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}=\sum_{i,j=1}^{m}\sum_{k,\ell=0}^{\infty}\frac{c_{k}c_{\ell}}{k!\ell!}\mathcal{C}_{k,\ell,3,3}^{i,j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}=\sum_{i,j=1}^{m}\sum_{k,\ell=0}^{\infty}\frac{c_{k}c_{\ell}}{k!\ell!}\mathcal{C}_{k,\ell,3,3}^{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{C}_{k,\ell,3,3}^{i,j}=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{C}_{k,\ell,3,3}^{i,j}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{He_{k}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{3}\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Although a large body of prior work has exploited the nice property of Hermite polynomial that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{He_{k}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{He_{k}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{He_{k}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}=k!\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\right)^{k}\mathbb{I}\left\{k=\ell\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{He_{k}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{He_{k}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{He_{k}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)He_{\ell}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}=k!\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\right)^{k}\mathbb{I}\left\{k=\ell\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\mathbf{u}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\mathbf{u}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with unit norm, in our setting we have to deal with the expectation of the product of four Hermite polynomial. Our main tool of handling this difficulty is the lemma below.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            We are not the first to introduce this result. However, we could not find a formal published source that proves the result.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},\bm{\Sigma}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},\bm{\Sigma}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. For some multi-index <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{k}}\in\mathbb{N}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{k}}\in\mathbb{N}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the multi-variate Hermite polynomial as</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="He_{{\mathbf{k}}}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}He_{{\mathbf{k}}[i]}\left({\mathbf{x}}[i]\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>𝐤</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">He_{{\mathbf{k}}}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}He_{{\mathbf{k}}[i]}\left({\mathbf{x}}[i]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then we have that for <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},\bm{\Sigma}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\bm{0},\bm{\Sigma}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{He_{{\mathbf{k}}}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{He_{{\mathbf{k}}}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{He_{{\mathbf{k}}}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}=\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}{\mathbf{k}}[i]!\right)\sum_{{\mathbf{M}}\in\mathcal{S}}\prod_{i,j=1}^{n}\frac{\bm{\Sigma}[i,j]^{{\mathbf{M}}[i,j]}}{{\mathbf{M}}[i,j]!}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>𝐤</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{He_{{\mathbf{k}}}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{He_{{\mathbf{k}}}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{He_{{\mathbf{k}}}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}=\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n}{\mathbf{k}}[i]!\right)\sum_{{\mathbf{M}}\in\mathcal{S}}\prod_{i,j=1}^{n}\frac{\bm{\Sigma}[i,j]^{{\mathbf{M}}[i,j]}}{{\mathbf{M}}[i,j]!}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the set <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of symmetric matrices <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{M}}\in\mathbb{N}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{M}}\in\mathbb{N}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{M}}[i,i]=0;\;\sum_{j=1}^{n}{\mathbf{M}}[i,j]={\mathbf{k}}[i];\;\;\forall i\in[n]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{M}}[i,i]=0;\;\sum_{j=1}^{n}{\mathbf{M}}[i,j]={\mathbf{k}}[i];\;\;\forall i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 4.1 Guided by the Experts: A Proof Sketch of Theorem ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, each <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{M}}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{M}}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> can be considered as the adjacency matrix of a graph with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> nodes and integer weights such that the degree of the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th node is <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{k}}[i]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐤</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{k}}[i]</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 4.1 Guided by the Experts: A Proof Sketch of Theorem ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to our case thus only requires to enumerate the graphs of four nodes with degree <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,\ell,3,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,\ell,3,3)</annotation></semantics></math>. With Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 4.1 Guided by the Experts: A Proof Sketch of Theorem ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we are able to derive the form of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{{\mathbf{v}}_{i}}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{{\mathbf{v}}_{i}}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{{\mathbf{w}}_{i}}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{{\mathbf{w}}_{i}}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, whose exact form are omitted from the main text due to its intricacy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient flow dynamic of target alignment.</span> Recall that our goal is to show that there is some <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that converges to <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[m^{\star}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[m^{\star}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, it is intuitive to start with tracking the dynamic of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. With gradient flow, we have that</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}\frac{d}{dt}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}=-\left\|{\mathbf{v}}_{i}(t)\right\|_{2}^{-1}\nabla_{{\mathbf{v}}_{i}}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\\
\frac{d}{dt}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}=-\left\|{\mathbf{w}}_{i}(t)\right\|_{2}^{-1}\nabla_{{\mathbf{w}}_{i}}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}\frac{d}{dt}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}=-\left\|{\mathbf{v}}_{i}(t)\right\|_{2}^{-1}\nabla_{{\mathbf{v}}_{i}}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\\
\frac{d}{dt}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}=-\left\|{\mathbf{w}}_{i}(t)\right\|_{2}^{-1}\nabla_{{\mathbf{w}}_{i}}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">due to the fact that <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|{\mathbf{v}}_{i}\right\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|{\mathbf{v}}_{i}\right\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|{\mathbf{w}}_{i}\right\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|{\mathbf{w}}_{i}\right\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> stays constant during the gradient flow process. Utilizing the fact that <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\ll 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\ll 1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are near their initialization, we can utilize Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 4.1 Guided by the Experts: A Proof Sketch of Theorem ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to approximate (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In 4.1 Guided by the Experts: A Proof Sketch of Theorem ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) as</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{d}{dt}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">The approximation above exhibits two interesting behaviors near initialization. First, the improvements in both the router alignment <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and the expert alignment <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the current magnitude of the expert alignment. This implies that, once the expert alignment reaches a magnitude of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\left(1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\left(1\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, it will take only constant time for the router and expert alignments to grow to a value near one (perfect alignment). This behavior can be observed in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> where the router alignment values (solid lines except for the gree on) follow closely as the expert alignment values (dashed lines) increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Second, the quadratic dependency in the expert alignment dynamic induces a sharp phase transition where the alignment value starts off slow for a period of time, and suddenly increases with a fast speed (see dashed lines in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), as studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. (2025)</a></cite>. This sharp phase transition is particularly helpful to prevent multiple experts from the student model to converge to the same expert in the teacher model. As an example, consider dynamics <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> with a small different <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> at initialization</p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq(1+\Delta)\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\leq\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\leq\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{d}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq(1+\Delta)\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\leq\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\leq\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Solving the ODE in (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4.1 Guided by the Experts: A Proof Sketch of Theorem ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) gives that for <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\approx\left(\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}-t\right)^{-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\approx\left(\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}-t\right)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, the time <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> required for <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\geq\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(t)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\geq\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}-2\leq\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}-2\leq\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, at time <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, one can compute that</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\left(\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}-\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}\right)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\left(\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}-\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right)^{-1}\right)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}}{\Delta}\leq\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{\Delta^{-1}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}}{\Delta}\leq\tilde{\mathcal{O}}\left(\frac{\Delta^{-1}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{d}\gg\Delta^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>≫</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{d}\gg\Delta^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can conclude that <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(T)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\geq\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{2}(T)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\geq\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(T)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\ll\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}(T)^{\top}{\mathbf{w}}_{j}^{\star}\ll\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This behavior implies that, the expert in the student model that aligns with expert <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in the teacher best at initialization will converge to some <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\left(1\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\left(1\right)</annotation></semantics></math> quickly while the other experts’ alignment remains small. Below, we formalize this dominance determined by the initialization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alignment gap at Initialization.</span> We show that there is a set of experts in the student model that aligns with each expert in the teacher model good enough to create a gap compared with other experts in the student model. At a high level, our goal here is to construct the mapping <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> based on the initialization. Our approach is a greedy forward selection similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In particular, we define
Define <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\ell}=\{i_{k}^{\star}\}_{k=1}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\ell}=\{i_{k}^{\star}\}_{k=1}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\ell}=\{j_{\ell}^{\star}\}_{k=1}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\ell}=\{j_{\ell}^{\star}\}_{k=1}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> recursively as follows</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{\ell+1}^{\star},j_{\ell+1}^{\star}=\argmax_{i\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{R}_{\ell},j\in[m^{\star}]\setminus\mathcal{C}_{\ell}}{\mathbf{w}}_{i}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{\ell+1}^{\star},j_{\ell+1}^{\star}=\argmax_{i\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{R}_{\ell},j\in[m^{\star}]\setminus\mathcal{C}_{\ell}}{\mathbf{w}}_{i}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">We expect that <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}\left(j_{\ell}^{\star}\right)=i_{\ell}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}\left(j_{\ell}^{\star}\right)=i_{\ell}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Namely, we expect <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j_{\ell}^{\star}}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j_{\ell}^{\star}}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j_{\ell}^{\star}}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j_{\ell}^{\star}}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> to converge to <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. The index <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the order of the sequential convergence. That is, we expect that <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j_{1}^{\star}}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j_{1}^{\star}}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> to grow large first, followed by <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j_{2}^{\star}}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j_{2}^{\star}}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, etc. Our theorem below shows that at initialization, the pairs <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j_{\ell}^{\star}}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j_{\ell}^{\star}}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> has a gap compared with other alignment values.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{1},\dots,{\mathbf{w}}_{m}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,d^{-1}{\mathbf{I}}_{d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{1},\dots,{\mathbf{w}}_{m}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,d^{-1}{\mathbf{I}}_{d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> be I.I.D. Gaussian random vectors. Define</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i_{\ell}^{\star},j_{\ell}^{\star}=\argmax_{i\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{R}_{\ell-1},j\in[m^{\star}]\setminus\mathcal{C}_{\ell-1}}{\mathbf{w}}_{i}[j]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i_{\ell}^{\star},j_{\ell}^{\star}=\argmax_{i\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{R}_{\ell-1},j\in[m^{\star}]\setminus\mathcal{C}_{\ell-1}}{\mathbf{w}}_{i}[j]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{\ell}=\{i_{k}^{\star}\}_{k=1}^{\ell};\;\;\mathcal{C}_{\ell}=\{j_{k}^{\star}\}_{k=1}^{\ell}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{\ell}=\{i_{k}^{\star}\}_{k=1}^{\ell};\;\;\mathcal{C}_{\ell}=\{j_{k}^{\star}\}_{k=1}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let any <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\in(0,\nicefrac{{1}}{{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\in(0,\nicefrac{{1}}{{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> be given. Then there exists some absolute constant <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2},\beta_{4}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2},\beta_{4}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that if <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\beta_{4}m^{\star}\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\beta_{4}m^{\star}\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then for <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{s}=\frac{\beta_{2}\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}{m^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{s}=\frac{\beta_{2}\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}{m^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-4\delta_{\mathbb{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-4\delta_{\mathbb{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Row-wise Gap) </span><math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j^{\star}_{\ell}]\geq\left(1+2\delta_{s}\right){\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j^{\star}_{\ell}]\geq\left(1+2\delta_{s}\right){\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[m^{\star}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[m^{\star}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[m^{\star}]\setminus\mathcal{C}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[m^{\star}]\setminus\mathcal{C}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Column-wise Gap) </span><math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j^{\star}_{\ell}]\geq\left(1+2\delta_{s}\right){\mathbf{w}}_{i}[j^{\star}_{\ell}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j^{\star}_{\ell}]\geq\left(1+2\delta_{s}\right){\mathbf{w}}_{i}[j^{\star}_{\ell}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[m^{\star}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[m^{\star}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{R}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{R}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Threshold Gap) </span><math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j^{\star}_{\ell}]\geq\left(1+2\delta_{s}\right){\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell+1}}[j^{\star}_{\ell+1}]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j^{\star}_{\ell}]\geq\left(1+2\delta_{s}\right){\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell+1}}[j^{\star}_{\ell+1}]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[m^{\star}-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[m^{\star}-1]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Magnitude Lower Bound) </span><math id="S4.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j_{\ell}^{\star}]^{2}\geq\frac{\log m^{\star}}{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{i^{\star}_{\ell}}[j_{\ell}^{\star}]^{2}\geq\frac{\log m^{\star}}{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S4.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[m]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Since the standard Gaussian distribution is rotational invariant, we can the gaps and lower bound shown in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 4.1 Guided by the Experts: A Proof Sketch of Theorem ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to the initial alignment scores <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(0)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Roughly speaking, the <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">row-wise gap</span> facilitates that <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> will not converge to <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>s with <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq j_{\ell}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq j_{\ell}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>; the <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">column-wise gap</span> induces the fact that no <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> will converge to <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j_{\ell}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>j</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j_{\ell}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> except for <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">threshold gap</span> leads to the sequential recovery as stated in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Finally, the <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">magnitude lower bound</span> guarantees that at the target alignment values at initialization are not too small for the whole convergence process to be too long.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Induction-based Proof.</span> With the goal of tracking the growth of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> in mind, however, we also have to track the “mis-alignments” including <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and “self-alignments” <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}(t),\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}(t),\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}(t),\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}(t),\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}(t)\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> due to the complicated form of the gradient, as can be seem from (<a href="#A1.E10" title="In A.3 Hermite Expansion of the Gradient and the Gradient Flow Dynamics ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) from the Appendix, so that their value does not interrupt with the target dynamics. To this end, our proof is an induction on <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[m^{\star}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[m^{\star}]</annotation></semantics></math> that assumes</p>
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{\ell^{\prime}}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{\ell^{\prime}}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell^{\prime}}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell^{\prime}}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> are close to one for <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\prime}&lt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\prime}&lt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the top-<math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math> router and experts are recovered well while the <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>th router-expert pair still remains not learned.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The “mis-alignments” and “self-alignments” associated with the recovered router-expert pairs must be small throughout the process.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">to show that <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{\ell}^{\star}}(t)^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> converge to a close-to-one value. A formal statement of the inductive hypothesis is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Proof Outline ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>, and the complete proof is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of Theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> guarantees that in <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> time, the student MoE model trained with gradient flow extracts <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>-pairs of near-perfect features from the teacher model. However, recall that the student have an over-parameterization of <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\Omega\left(m^{\star}\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\Omega\left(m^{\star}\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Despite being moderate, the <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\frac{m^{\star}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}</annotation></semantics></math> factor still leads to a large number of excessive parameters. Continuing to train these unused experts together with their corresponding router parameter results in a wast of the computation resource, regardless of whether they can converge to zero output or not. This theoretical insight corresponds with existing empirical works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025d</a>)</cite> which discovers the existence of redundant experts in pre-trained LLMs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Pruning the Redundant Experts</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In this section, we adopt a greedy pruning algorithm based on the test loss similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. (2024)</a></cite> to remove the redundant experts, and show that, if we apply the algorithm at <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\star}\leq t\leq T^{\star}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\sqrt{d}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}m^{2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\star}\leq t\leq T^{\star}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{\sqrt{d}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}m^{2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, then we can provably remove all the unused experts and keep all the correctly learned router-expert pairs as stated in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. To state the algorithm, we first define the sub-model MoE induced by <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq[m]</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{i\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{S}}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{i\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{S}}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We consider the following pruning procedure that iteratively constructs the pruned set <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>th step, we identify an index <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\tau}\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{S}_{\tau-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\tau}\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{S}_{\tau-1}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}r_{\tau}=\argmin_{r\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{S}_{\tau-1}}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f_{S_{\tau-1}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f_{S_{\tau-1}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f_{S_{\tau-1}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}\\
\mathcal{S}_{\tau}=\mathcal{S}_{\tau-1}\cup\left\{r_{\tau}\right\}\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}r_{\tau}=\argmin_{r\in[m]\setminus\mathcal{S}_{\tau-1}}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f_{S_{\tau-1}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f_{S_{\tau-1}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f_{S_{\tau-1}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}\\
\mathcal{S}_{\tau}=\mathcal{S}_{\tau-1}\cup\left\{r_{\tau}\right\}\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The procedure will stop when pruning one more expert does not improve the population loss. In particular, we define the stopping step <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> be such that</p>
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{r\in[m]}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f_{S_{\tau^{\star}}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f_{S_{\tau^{\star}}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f_{S_{\tau^{\star}}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{r\in[m]}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f_{S_{\tau^{\star}}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f_{S_{\tau^{\star}}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f_{S_{\tau^{\star}}\cup\{r\}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f_{\mathcal{S}_{\tau^{\star}}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f_{\mathcal{S}_{\tau^{\star}}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f_{\mathcal{S}_{\tau^{\star}}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="3.278em">≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(f_{\mathcal{S}_{\tau^{\star}}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(f_{\mathcal{S}_{\tau^{\star}}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(f_{\mathcal{S}_{\tau^{\star}}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-f^{\star}\left({\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">For the simplicity of the analysis, we assume that <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(i)=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(i)=i</annotation></semantics></math>, as reordering the router-expert pairs does not change <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. To facilitate the analysis of the pruning procedure, we make the following assumption</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmasump2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_asump">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmasump2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmasump2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmasump2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmasump2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasump2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmasump2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{{\mathbf{v}}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{{\mathbf{v}}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmasump2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{{\mathbf{w}}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{{\mathbf{w}}_{i}\right\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the router and expert weights of the MoE model in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Let <math id="Thmasump2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\}_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\}_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmasump2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\}_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\}_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the router and expert weights of the teacher model in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). There exists <math id="Thmasump2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\leq o\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{m}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\leq o\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{m}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmasump2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m^{\star}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m^{\star}]</annotation></semantics></math> it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\left\{\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\}\geq 1-\varepsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\left\{\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\}\geq 1-\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmasump2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmasump2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for all <math id="Thmasump2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{1}\in[m]\setminus[m^{\star}],i_{2}\in[m],i_{1}\neq i_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{1}\in[m]\setminus[m^{\star}],i_{2}\in[m],i_{1}\neq i_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmasump2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[m^{\star}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[m^{\star}]</annotation></semantics></math> it holds that</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{2}}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{2}}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}\right|\leq\varepsilon" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{2}}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{2}}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{2}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i_{1}}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i_{1}}\right|\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|\leq\varepsilon" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|,\left|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{j}^{\star}\right|\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right),\mathcal{S}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{S}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right),\mathcal{S}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> be defined above. If Assumption <a href="#Thmasump2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 5.1 Pruning the Redundant Experts ‣ 5 PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> holds, then we have that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{S}_{\tau^{\star}}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\vskip-5.69046pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{S}_{\tau^{\star}}}\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\vskip-5.69046pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5.1 Pruning the Redundant Experts ‣ 5 PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> states that, after <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> steps of pruning, the resulting model contains the exact <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> router-expert pairs with learned features from the teacher model. As a condition of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5.1 Pruning the Redundant Experts ‣ 5 PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, Assumption <a href="#Thmasump2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 5.1 Pruning the Redundant Experts ‣ 5 PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is satisfied by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)\leq o\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{m}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>m</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)\leq o\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{m}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\gg m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\gg m</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that, if we perform the pruning at <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\star}\leq t\leq T^{\star}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\star}\leq t\leq T^{\star}+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in the gradient flow process, we are guaranteed to remove all unused router-expert pairs and keep all necessary ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that in the pruning procedure we evaluate the model on the population loss. To apply the algorithm in practice, one can effectively approximate the population loss with the sample loss. We use the population loss for the succinctness of the theoretical analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sketch of Proof.</span> From a high level perspective, our proof relies on the observation that for two nonlinear function <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1},h_{2}:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1},h_{2}:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and vectors <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\mathbf{u}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\mathbf{u}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that</p>
<table id="S5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\approx\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="3.278em">≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad\approx\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{h_{1}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right.}{h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{h_{2}\left({\mathbf{u}}_{2}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. This allows us to approximate the loss as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}\left(\theta\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}\left(\theta\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\left(q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star},{\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\left(q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star},{\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\left(q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star},{\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\left(q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star},{\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)\right)^{2}}\right.}{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\left(q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star},{\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\left(\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\left(q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i},{\mathbf{x}}\right)-q\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star},{\mathbf{x}}\right)\right)\right)^{2}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad+\sum_{i=m^{\star}+1}^{m}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mi>σ</mi><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\quad\quad+\sum_{i=m^{\star}+1}^{m}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{{\mathbf{x}}}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right.}{\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)^{2}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The first term is naturally small due to the fact that <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> are close to one for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m^{\star}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m^{\star}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The second term involves a summation of positive terms, which depends on the redundant router-expert pairs. Thus, removing each one of these will decrease <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 5.1 Pruning the Redundant Experts ‣ 5 PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> is provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proof of Theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Fine-Tuning the Pruned Model</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that, although the <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> router-expert pairs in the student model extracted near-perfect features from the teacher model, there is still an <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{7}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{3}d^{\frac{3}{2}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{7}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{3}d^{\frac{3}{2}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> error for each <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{\mathcal{I}(i)}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. This results in a non-zero loss even after the pruning in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Pruning the Redundant Experts ‣ 5 PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.
In this section, we study the convergence guarantee of fine-tuning the pruned model with gradient flow on the population MSE. In particular, we assume that <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the pruned model from Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Pruning the Redundant Experts ‣ 5 PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> given by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\vskip-5.69046pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\left(\bm{\theta},{\mathbf{x}}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{m^{\star}}\pi\left(\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\sigma\left(\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\right)\vskip-5.69046pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the fine-tuning starts at <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}(T_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}(T_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> learned from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> at time <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\star}\leq T_{0}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{4}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{2}d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\star}\leq T_{0}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{4}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}^{2}d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We slightly abuse the notation by denoting</p>
<table id="S5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}=[{\mathbf{v}}_{1}^{\top},\dots,{\mathbf{v}}_{m^{\star}}^{\top},{\mathbf{w}}_{1}^{\top},\dots,{\mathbf{w}}_{m^{\star}}^{\top}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{2m^{\star}d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}=[{\mathbf{v}}_{1}^{\top},\dots,{\mathbf{v}}_{m^{\star}}^{\top},{\mathbf{w}}_{1}^{\top},\dots,{\mathbf{w}}_{m^{\star}}^{\top}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{2m^{\star}d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we denote the normalized version of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\bm{\theta}}=[\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{1}^{\top},\dots,\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{m^{\star}}^{\top},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}^{\top},\dots,\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{m^{\star}}^{\top}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{2m^{\star}d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\bm{\theta}}=[\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{1}^{\top},\dots,\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{m^{\star}}^{\top},\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{1}^{\top},\dots,\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{m^{\star}}^{\top}]^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{2m^{\star}d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem shows the convergence of gradient flow in the fine-tuning phase.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}(T_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}(T_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfy <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\leq o\left(\frac{1}{m^{\star 2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\leq o\left(\frac{1}{m^{\star 2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{S,0}=2\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{x\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi\left(x\right)^{2}}\right.}{\pi\left(x\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi\left(x\right)^{2}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{S,0}=2\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{x\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi\left(x\right)^{2}}\right.}{\pi\left(x\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi\left(x\right)^{2}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{S,1}=6\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{x\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi^{\prime}\left(x\right)^{2}}\right.}{\pi^{\prime}\left(x\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi^{\prime}\left(x\right)^{2}}\right]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{S,1}=6\mathrm{\mathbb{E}}_{x\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)}\boldsymbol{\left[\vphantom{\pi^{\prime}\left(x\right)^{2}}\right.}{\pi^{\prime}\left(x\right)^{2}}\boldsymbol{\left.\vphantom{\pi^{\prime}\left(x\right)^{2}}\right]}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{S,0}\geq 1.1C_{S,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{S,0}\geq 1.1C_{S,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists some constant <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> that only depends on the property of the sigmoid function <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\left(\cdot\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\left(\cdot\right)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bar{\bm{\theta}}\left(t+T_{0}\right)-\bm{\theta}^{\star}\right\|_{2}^{2}\leq\text{exp}\left(-\frac{\kappa t}{2}\right)\left\|\bar{\bm{\theta}}(T_{0})-\bm{\theta}^{\star}\right\|_{2}^{2}\vskip-5.69046pt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">exp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bar{\bm{\theta}}\left(t+T_{0}\right)-\bm{\theta}^{\star}\right\|_{2}^{2}\leq\text{exp}\left(-\frac{\kappa t}{2}\right)\left\|\bar{\bm{\theta}}(T_{0})-\bm{\theta}^{\star}\right\|_{2}^{2}\vskip-5.69046pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Under the condition that <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>’s and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>’s, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 5.2 Fine-Tuning the Pruned Model ‣ 5 PRUNING AND FINE-TUNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows a linear convergence rate in terms of the difference between the pruned model’s normalized parameters <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the optimal parameters <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this fine-tuning stage, the convergence rate <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the dimension <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> or the number of experts <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. Instead, it only depends on the property of the router’s non-linear function <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}\left(T_{0}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}\left(T_{0}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the learned result in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4 MAIN RESULT: SEQUENTIAL FEATURE LEARNING" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the condition that <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m}{\sqrt{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}d^{\frac{1}{4}}}\right)\leq o\left(\frac{1}{m^{\star 2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m}{\sqrt{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}}d^{\frac{1}{4}}}\right)\leq o\left(\frac{1}{m^{\star 2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> are automatically satisfied under <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\gg m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\gg m</annotation></semantics></math>, since</p>
<table id="S5.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}^{2}=2-2\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}^{2}=2-2\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\top}\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{m^{2}}{\delta_{\mathbb{P}}\sqrt{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The assumption that <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{S,0}\geq 1.1C_{S,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1.1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{S,0}\geq 1.1C_{S,1}</annotation></semantics></math> only depends on the property of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and is checked in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Plotting Sigmoid Property" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sketch of Proof.</span> Our proof relies on the idea that, near the global minimum, the Hessian matrix is positive definite. In particular, we show that for any vector <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{2m^{\star}d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{2m^{\star}d}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\left\langle{\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\right\rangle\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\left\langle{\mathbf{u}}_{1},{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\right\rangle\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}\nabla^{2}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right){\mathbf{u}}_{2}\geq\kappa\left\|{\mathbf{u}}_{1}\right\|_{2}\left\|{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\right\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{u}}_{1}^{\top}\nabla^{2}{\mathcal{L}}\left(\bm{\theta}\right){\mathbf{u}}_{2}\geq\kappa\left\|{\mathbf{u}}_{1}\right\|_{2}\left\|{\mathbf{u}}_{2}\right\|</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}-\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}\right\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\leq o\left(\frac{1}{m^{\star 2}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\leq o\left(\frac{1}{m^{\star 2}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4. ‣ Appendix C Proof of Theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Proof of Theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> provides a formal statement of the result. Based on the positive-definiteness of the Hessian matrix, we leverage the classic convex optimization technique to show that the trajectory never leaves the neighborhood near the global minima, and that the distance to the global minimum converges linearly. The full proof is deferred to Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Proof of Theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>CONCLUSION</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the teacher-student set-up, we study the learning dynamics of the sigmoid-routed MoE with nonlinear experts defined in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 PRELIMINARY AND SET-UP" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) when trained with gradient flow on the population MSE over high dimensional Gaussian inputs. In particular, our main result is a characterization of the feature learning stage, where proper features of the router-expert pairs are discovered in sequential order, with the expert’s recovery leading the router’s recovery. At the end of the feature learning stage, we show that a pruning procedure can be conducted to provably remove all the redundant experts and keep all necessary ones. Lastly, we show a linear convergence rate to the global minima for the the post-pruning fine-tuning with gradient flow. To the best of our knowledge, our work is the first to provide theoretical understanding on the joint training guarantee of MoEs with more than one activated experts and a general data assumption. In general, our paper is a further step into understanding the complicated dynamics of MoE training, and leads to the following open problems:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Online SGD and Sample Complexity.</span> Due to the already sophisticated proof, our study is restricted to the setting of gradient flow on the population loss. However, as the main idea of the proof consists of an ODE based dynamic analysis, one could discretize the dynamic and apply martingale-based analysis to extend the theory to online SGD, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. (2025)</a></cite>. This extension may lead to a sample complexity bound of learning <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> experts on <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experts with different importance.</span> In our work we considered the teacher’s router and expert parameter <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{v}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{{\mathbf{w}}}_{i}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> to be an orthonormal list. Due to the rotational invariance, this set-up puts equal importance to each router-expert pairs. Future work can investigate the scenario where the <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th expert is scaled with a factor of <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and study the explicit ordering of the recovered experts in the student model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relax the dependency of <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> Our current theory relies on the fact that <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\gg m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≫</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\gg m</annotation></semantics></math>. While in the practical application of MoE we rarely set the number of experts to be larger than the input dimension, in most cases the scale of the two remains relatively the same. A meaningful future direction is to bridge the gap by studying the setting where <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is only moderately larger than <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
